<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Common Box Room Mistakes</h2>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Buying a bed that is too big.</strong> A small double can leave no room to walk or open drawers.</li>



<li><strong>Furniture on every wall.</strong> Keep at least one wall mostly clear.</li>



<li><strong>Ignoring the door swing.</strong> Check the door opens fully past the bed and wardrobe.</li>



<li><strong>Loading the stair bulkhead.</strong> Ask a joiner before you build on it or stand on it.</li>



<li><strong>Dark, heavy colours everywhere.</strong> They can make a room with little daylight feel smaller.</li>



<li><strong>Letting it become a junk room.</strong> Give every stored item a box, a shelf or a drawer.</li>
</ul>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Box Room FAQ</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Can you fit a double bed in a box room?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Sometimes, but it is usually a squeeze. A small double (120 x 190 cm) can work in a room about 2.4 m wide that is also long enough for the bed plus the door swing, but you may lose the walkway and space for drawers. In most box rooms a single bed, a cabin bed or a high sleeper works better.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">How big is a box room in the UK?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">There is no fixed size. Many box rooms are around 2 to 2.5 metres on each side. For new homes in England, the Nationally Described Space Standard asks for single bedrooms of at least 7.5 m² and 2.15 m wide, and many older box rooms are smaller than that.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">How do you make a box room look bigger?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Keep the floor as clear as possible, use a bed with storage underneath, build storage up the walls, choose pale matching colours and add a mirror that reflects the window. Wall lights and floating shelves instead of bedside tables also help.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">What can you do with the bulkhead in a box room?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">You can build a wardrobe, shelf or small desk over it, or simply leave it as a surface for baskets. Bulkheads are often a light timber frame over the stairs, so ask a joiner before adding weight, and do not use it as a step or seat unless it has been checked.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If the box room is in the roof, or you are running out of space everywhere, our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/loft-storage-ideas/">loft storage ideas</a> cover eaves drawers, eaves cupboards and how to board a loft safely.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Final Thoughts</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The best box room bedroom ideas use height instead of floor: a bed with storage or a high sleeper, shelves and wardrobes up the walls and smart use of the stair bulkhead. Pick one clear purpose for the room, plan the layout first and keep at least one wall mostly clear. If your house has other awkward spaces, see our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/under-stairs-storage-ideas/">under stairs storage ideas</a> and <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/airing-cupboard-shelves/">airing cupboard shelves</a> guides.</p>



